You're not born with it, you learn these
skills as you go through it.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:07.930
And I just wanna focus on a couple of
them.

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:10.900
On the maybe the what to do it might be
different.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:13.800
I maybe I'll just I'll pick on two.

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:15.480
One is the telescope and microscope.

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:19.940
And I've heard this from many leaders
today that say basically in

00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:24.540
this world, that's very volatile, yet
there's these big secular trends going on.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.970
And I, I learned this actually from, the
Minister of

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:31.730
Finance, in Canada, Jim Flaherty, during
the, the, the financial crisis.

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:34.590
And he said, I need to, be use to have a
telescope

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:37.750
in one eye, and a microscope in the other
without getting a headache.

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:40.030
And it, I don't know if anyone's tried
that.

00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:41.810
You put a microscope in one eye.

00:27:41.810 --> 00:27:43.860
It's not a very pleasant experience.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:45.800
You can't focus.

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.960
But the point is you need to because you
have to have

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:54.740
a short term microscopic view because
there's so many volatile things happening.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:55.860
You could be out of business.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:58.270
You could, you, because of risks.

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:00.160
Because of a new competitor or whatever
that you want to

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:03.300
say, that you have to manage for the very
short term.

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:05.700
The reputational issues, there is so many
issues going on.

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:10.420
But at the same time you have to think
long term about where you want to be.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:13.350
And so if I translate that just to
McKensey.

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.810
We are finding out in the work we do we
are

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:21.620
doing a lot more literally, 30 day
scenario plans for companies.

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:25.370
Like what are we gonna be able to do to
make sure we're agile as a company.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:28.775
Cause you, if you're a big institution
like a

00:28:28.775 --> 00:28:33.590
Wal-Mart, or a Procter and Gamble, or a
GlaxoSmithKline Beecham.

00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:37.440
You have to, you have to be able to run
like hell as an elephant.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.540
You can't sort of trundle.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:41.910
You have, how do you move very quickly.

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:45.780
Because you literally can be out of
business in a six month

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:50.340
period if you're not careful enough.I can
register the stories you know well.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:55.800
Look at Best Buy, which I think is, is
doing well now, but look at the challenge.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:59.200
And they were the darling of the industry
only two years ago.

00:28:59.200 --> 00:29:01.420
You look at, you look at the you look

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:05.330
at what's happening with Nokia, Apple,
Samsung and so forth.

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:07.850
And the paranoia that's in there about how
quickly.

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:10.910
So the short term is important, but we're
also doing

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:13.310
not those 30 day views, we're also doing
2020 views.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:18.000
Which is, that may be the case, but where
do we want to be in 2020?

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:20.920
What should our footprint look like?

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:22.390
What should our talent look like?

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:26.920
If you believe that billion new middle
class consumers in three

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:31.920
billion, what, how many of those people
should be on my board?

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:35.870
You know, how many Chinese, Nigerians,
Brazilians, do

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:37.980
I have on my board as a multinational
company?

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:41.270
How, what percent of my top 100 comes from
that area?

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:44.660
What proportion of my investments are
being done in that area?

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:47.660
So you need the short term and the long
term at the same time.

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:50.688
The other one I'd say is tri sector
athlete.

00:29:50.688 --> 00:29:54.242
I think again if I could do my career over
in McKinsey, I

00:29:54.242 --> 00:29:59.517
wished I had more experience working in
the public sector and the social sector.

00:29:59.517 --> 00:30:02.333
If you want to be a private sector, hard
core,

00:30:02.333 --> 00:30:07.880
capitalist, business, shareholder value
driven person, that's all you wanna do.

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:12.130
You will not be successful in my view
unless you've had experience with

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:15.850
the public sector and the social sector
and if you wanna be a very

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:20.680
successful public sector leader, I think
you have to have experience in the

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:24.330
private sector and the social sector, and
the same goes for the social sector.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:27.510
If you want to be a great social sector
leader you need that, and

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:30.740
that's this tri-sector athlete notion
which Joe

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:33.100
and I has has come up with, before.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:37.350
And that's something that we've actually 3
McKenzie people have left the firm

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:43.390
to set up a tri-sector athlete, forum,
that they're running out of Washington.

00:30:43.390 --> 00:30:44.670
And, the notion here is just to

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:47.330
get experiences working in these different
places

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:49.810
because you have to understand the
mindset,

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:51.318
of the challenges that are going on.

00:30:51.318 --> 00:30:53.311
I would and, I could go on, on and on

00:30:53.311 --> 00:30:55.555
and on that, but, I think, thinking of
those experiences.

00:30:55.555 --> 00:30:58.800
So in McKinsey, for example, our public

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:01.560
sector practice and social sector practice
are absolutely

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:03.620
critical in my view for our leadership

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:07.410
development, right, for people to have
that experience.

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:10.260
And we're very encouraging of people
leaving to go do

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.860
that for a couple of years, and then come
back.

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:15.640
If, you know, we'd like them to come
sometimes and they stay and

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:20.000
go to, we need much more, circulation if
you will that's that's gong on.

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:23.368
So those are just two comments I'd make on
the, on the,

00:31:23.368 --> 00:31:27.420
on what is it that people do and the
capabilities they have.

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:30.830
And the other part I would just say is on
personal attributes.

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:36.680
This is the character and the strong sense
of purpose, the calm in

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:39.920
the, in the eye of the hurricane just
maybe two stories on that.

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:43.500
On sense of purpose, what one thing we've
discovered, is a

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:48.020
lot of CEOs saying, we notice there's more
right vs right decisions.

00:31:48.020 --> 00:31:53.590
Right, at, these are the decisions where
It, it's if you, both

00:31:53.590 --> 00:31:58.820
decisions are good outcomes the problem is
one is negatively effects the other.

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:01.250
So how do you, it's easy to make a
decision when

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:06.910
you go I can make more money by doing this
versus that.

00:32:06.910 --> 00:32:11.220
What happens if their situations which
they are about your culture,

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:14.960
or your values and I'll give you an
example, Howard Shultz.

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:19.250
You know, he was thinking about what he
was doing at Starbucks and

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:22.130
he had the view we need to have the next
generation of leadership.

00:32:22.130 --> 00:32:26.678
It's very important that we go down a
generation, get young people to be able to

00:32:26.678 --> 00:32:30.525
drive to the place and move it forward, I
feel very strongly about how that moves.

00:32:30.525 --> 00:32:33.900
And what happens when this current young
CEO's

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:37.350
not performing very well and he reinserts
him self?

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:41.020
That's a right versus right, cause he's
breaking the cultural norm

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:44.270
he wanted to set, but for the business he
thinks it's critical.

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:45.900
And I'm not, it's probably not a very good
example, but I'm

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:49.240
just trying to, there are example, where
there are more moral examples.

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:52.610
I'll give you another example Fonterra
which is the dairy

00:32:52.610 --> 00:32:55.710
is one of the largest dairy producers in
the world.

00:32:55.710 --> 00:33:01.430
What if your researchers come to you and
say, our, our cows can produce more milk

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:06.310
if we abort fetuses on a regular basis,
and you could say well they're animals.

00:33:06.310 --> 00:33:07.800
I mean, so what?

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:10.180
We just sort of move that, we, we just,
that's a good way,

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:13.210
it's a way to get more milk production
that's a good thing to do.

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:15.150
And, actually, there is, actually a little
bit of

00:33:15.150 --> 00:33:17.660
that's not a decision that you just jump
into.

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:19.717
And if you talk to the CEO of Fonterra,

00:33:19.717 --> 00:33:22.010
he'll say, I'll get one of those a week,
right?

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:24.770
They're slightly, they're not just
decisions where

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:27.420
you move, they're right versus right
decisions.

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:31.950
More moral decisions that one has to think
about, and what you're doing.

00:33:31.950 --> 00:33:33.450
And so it's very important to think in

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:36.550
your organization about what you stand
for, and

00:33:36.550 --> 00:33:38.280
how you base yourself and where you're
moving,

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.610
or you could find yourself moving, off
course.

00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:42.950
The, last wanna just say calm in the

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:45.110
eye of the hurricane, because of all these
volatility.

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:46.710
This is the last story I'll end up with
this.

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:49.650
When I meet many CEOs, and there's one

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:53.080
fellow running a very large global
insurance company.

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.480
I remember meeting him and I said, I said,
I

00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:59.480
always ask them what do you wish you'd
learned at the

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:02.010
beginning of your career that you know
now, after sort of

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:05.070
ten years being a CEO, what do you wish
you learned?

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:08.740
What this guy said was I wish I'd learned
about compartmentalization.

00:34:08.740 --> 00:34:10.240
And I said what does that mean?

00:34:10.240 --> 00:34:12.640
And he said well, if I'd met you in my

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:15.960
first few days as being CEO, I would have
kicked you

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:17.600
out of my office within 5 minutes and, I
said

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:19.650
does it, why did I offend, is it something
about me?

00:34:19.650 --> 00:34:22.870
And he goes no, cuz in my first few days
on the job

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:25.960
I, my general console had come in
literally when I was meeting I

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:31.180
was actually someone from Goldman Sachs
and they said we've been sued for

00:34:31.180 --> 00:34:35.740
six and a half billion dollars, and we're
probably gonna lose it, the lawsuit.

00:34:35.740 --> 00:34:39.230
And he said that, I couldn't concentrate
any more after that, like I, oh, I said,

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:42.560
it was, my god, how are we gonna deal with
this, and so I became obsessed.

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:44.890
He goes, when I'm talking to you right
now, I have six

00:34:44.890 --> 00:34:48.810
of those types of things that are like
plates spinning behind my head.

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:51.860
They're big issues, but, I'm focused on
you,

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:54.810
I'm not, I'm not distracted by those 6
things.

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:59.070
And, that takes that takes some agility in
your brain, to be able

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:03.900
to stay focused and, then handle some
weird things that are going on.

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:05.250
That's kind of the, the way it is.

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:06.830
I asked, I asked him, how do you learn
that?

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:08.930
I said, is that, how could you learn to
be,

00:35:09.940 --> 00:35:13.560
compartmentalized and resilient, and he
said I, I don't know.

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:16.410
He said, but I'll tell you something where
I have learned about it is at West Point.

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:17.740
And I said really?

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:20.230
The military, and he goes yeah, he goes
what they do with

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:23.570
the plead class, or at least it was one
session that he went

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:27.630
to, is they would say, we're gonna give
you an engineering problem, which

00:35:27.630 --> 00:35:30.030
is how to build a bridge that's been
destroyed, you've got 30 minutes.

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:33.720
It's more a mathematics problem but at the
same time

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:37.430
you do that you're gonna be crawling under
barbed wire

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.570
with what the cadets think is live machine
gun fire,

00:35:40.570 --> 00:35:42.470
so you don't kind of want to raise your
head.

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:46.540
So while you are solving the math problem,
you got to crawl under the barb wire.

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:47.870
And I was thinking I'd probably put that
into

00:35:47.870 --> 00:35:51.380
a McKenzie training program so we get
people going.

00:35:51.380 --> 00:35:56.710
But these notions of, again, of character
that sort of what's your, your purpose.

00:35:56.710 --> 00:35:59.190
Again, I would urge, we were talking about
this before.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:01.330
Read Clayton Christianson stuff.

00:36:01.330 --> 00:36:05.360
Not about innovation, but about how one
measures one's life.

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:09.890
It's a very worthwhile, his HBR article or
the actual book that he's

00:36:09.890 --> 00:36:14.170
just written, just on, if you wanna learn
more about that just on purpose.

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:17.920
But there's a lot around character,
resilience.

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:19.850
And, the last thing I'll say, and I
promise I'll

00:36:19.850 --> 00:36:22.740
shut up, is, one thing we've learned in
the McKinsey.

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:26.970
And we look at people in McKinsey, who
have more successful careers

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:28.820
that's by the way, outside McKinsey,

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:33.150
not just inside McKinsey, versus their
cohorts.

00:36:33.150 --> 00:36:36.210
What we found is, the more successful
group has

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:39.960
had more bad luck, than the less
successful group.

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:41.781
And, that seems a bit counter-intuitive,
right, how could

00:36:41.781 --> 00:36:44.610
that be the case, the more successful
people have more

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:48.070
bad luck than less successful and the
reason is

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:51.180
as you know they've taken more swings at
the bat.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:53.980
They've taken more risks and they failed.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:58.290
They've actually failed more but they get
up off their ass and

00:36:58.290 --> 00:37:01.710
they keep moving, they don't stop, they,
they keep they keep moving.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:04.400
And one of the things I think that's very
important

00:37:04.400 --> 00:37:07.130
on the character side is how can we give
people

00:37:07.130 --> 00:37:11.580
many chances to try things, recognizing
that they're gonna fail

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:14.100
and then allow them to, to keep, to keep
moving forward.

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:17.920
We found that in other industries and, and
professions as well.

00:37:17.920 --> 00:37:22.030
So, a time of historic change a time for

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:24.945
new type of leadership and, in what you're
doing.

00:37:24.945 --> 00:37:29.418
Cuz as I said again, I'm jealous for you
guys because

00:37:29.418 --> 00:37:36.600
you're gonna be leading in this, in

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:42.325
these historic times, I'll shut up right
there and why don't we go to questions.

