00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:25.630
They're trying to, because of just the
scale of where that's going.

00:40:25.630 --> 00:40:28.240
I think education is gonna be a

00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:31.800
massive business because of this lifetime
a learning.

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:34.120
I think the transformation about how
education

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:36.740
is done, especially at the junior levels.

00:40:36.740 --> 00:40:41.270
We were talking about German Gref from
Sberbank, if you get a chance to hear him.

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:44.410
His view is that the bulk of our
educational money

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:47.810
should be focused on preschool, cuz he
thinks that, he, he

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:49.680
believes in a lot of the science that is
the, you

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:54.350
know, your brain is basically formed by
the time you're six.

00:40:54.350 --> 00:40:56.860
So that's where we should be putting 80%
of our money.

00:40:56.860 --> 00:40:59.630
And, and he's actually building businesses
on, on this side of it.

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:01.360
I don't know whether the science is
exactly right

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:03.070
or not but I'm just saying that that's a
change.

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:07.490
Healthcare, which I said again is a
backward business from a consumer side.

00:41:07.490 --> 00:41:11.560
I, I could, I could list off 50 different
businesses.

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:15.210
If I get fired from McKinsey, I just,
there's so many

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:18.720
opportunities just on the health care side
of what could be done.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:24.340
So I think it's a time of huge
entrepreneurial opportunity.

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:26.110
And for McKinsey I think the issue is we

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:29.750
need to make sure that were working not
with the

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:32.090
existing large players and by the way
they're important

00:41:32.090 --> 00:41:35.880
because they're changing they're not
dying, they're trying to move.

00:41:35.880 --> 00:41:38.440
Bu it's very important that we work with
small players.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:42.233
So when I was in Davos for example I made
it a point this time, I'd

00:41:42.233 --> 00:41:44.800
basically met with organizations that had
a market

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:48.250
capital of less than $200 million dollars
right?

00:41:48.250 --> 00:41:51.140
And if I look at some of them in Africa
for example, I met

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:55.740
a bank CEO, this was a little larger, they
put a billion dollar market cap.

00:41:55.740 --> 00:41:58.890
They remind me of Indian banks in the mid
1990s.

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:01.660
So what we're ready to do is work

00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:06.050
with smaller institutions that are more
faster paced.

00:42:06.050 --> 00:42:11.020
We also have to be able to work with large
organizations to

00:42:11.020 --> 00:42:15.360
help them basically build their attackers
right, in terms of how they're operating.

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:20.140
And the way we work with clients I don't,
our clients don't just want a project

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:22.210
teams of people to just come in and

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:25.700
serve them, sometimes they just want data,
right?

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:28.658
So we're doing a lot of work in healthcare
right now.

00:42:28.658 --> 00:42:31.880
Where the hospital group wants our
benchmark

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:34.810
data which is proprietary proprietary that
we built

00:42:34.810 --> 00:42:37.150
and then have some people look and help,

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:39.090
advise them on how to use that
information.

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:43.380
So we're now, we probable acquired I guess
four data, proprietary

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:46.700
data companies in the last year as we
build that out.

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:49.660
And I, l don't think we're moving fast
enough on, on that side.

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:53.318
So I, I think there's, I think there's a
time of

00:42:53.318 --> 00:42:58.321
huge opportunity and change in terms of
the businesses to be build.

00:42:58.321 --> 00:42:58.343
yeah.

00:42:58.343 --> 00:43:03.180
Go here and then.

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:05.395
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the interesting talk.

00:43:05.395 --> 00:43:09.864
How do you see the government structure
changing in organizations?

00:43:09.864 --> 00:43:13.738
Do you still see Hierarchy in 2050 or any

00:43:13.738 --> 00:43:17.833
thoughts on that and then also the income
gap.

00:43:17.833 --> 00:43:21.805
So today the CEO is 300 x of the average
worker in the

00:43:21.805 --> 00:43:26.520
US in terms of do you see that shifting or
will remain the same?

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:27.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah I, I think we're definitely

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:32.010
gonna see flatter organizations, or
multileader organizations.

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:35.630
One of the last books that Marvin Bower's
really

00:43:35.630 --> 00:43:38.990
the founder of McKinsey, was called the
Leadership Organization.

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:41.860
His view was that a partnership model

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:44.360
because in McKinsey, I'm not really the
leader.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:47.420
I'd like to say I was no one, none of the
partners would really believe that.

00:43:47.420 --> 00:43:51.060
Everyone is a partner is out there doing
what they wanna do.

00:43:51.060 --> 00:43:53.140
In fact, the reason I joined McKinsey is I
didn't want to be

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:55.770
told what to do, and if someone did tell
me what to do,

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:59.450
it's kinda like the hair on the back of
your neck goes up

00:43:59.450 --> 00:44:04.470
because I wanna have the freedom to build
whatever practice I wanna do.

00:44:04.470 --> 00:44:08.130
And I think in, as I mentioned in these
organizations where there

00:44:08.130 --> 00:44:09.600
was this model of the one CEO that has all
the views.

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:12.950
With that complexity one person can't,
there's no way you can handle that.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:17.250
So I think we're gonna see much broader
leadership groups

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:21.720
where you have more ownership together in,
in the organization.

00:44:21.720 --> 00:44:25.530
And we're seeing that happening in
organizations where

00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:29.590
you have more leaders that they may have

00:44:29.590 --> 00:44:31.670
an area of specialization but they have a

00:44:31.670 --> 00:44:34.230
common ownership of, of what they need to
do.

00:44:34.230 --> 00:44:37.120
So I think we're going to see flatter,
broader leadership

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:41.220
structures, in terms of, of companies and
where they are.

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:45.290
And related to that, too, I do think the
income gap is gonna shift.

00:44:45.290 --> 00:44:48.161
I think that if there are some, vital
parts of

00:44:48.161 --> 00:44:52.405
organizations that may be six levels down,
in the organization.

00:44:52.405 --> 00:44:54.625
And again, I don't want to speak for
Apple,

00:44:54.625 --> 00:44:56.904
but if you're the person who, is building
the

00:44:56.904 --> 00:44:59.358
curves, on the tablets, and so forth,
that's a

00:44:59.358 --> 00:45:02.884
pretty critical, they're doing the
technology on that side, that.

00:45:02.884 --> 00:45:04.776
That may be creating a huge amount of

00:45:04.776 --> 00:45:08.258
the value that's actually occurring in the
organization.

00:45:08.258 --> 00:45:10.744
I think people are gonna recognize
specialty and

00:45:10.744 --> 00:45:14.250
capability more, so that you're gonna see
more variation.

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:16.210
In, in many views it's kinda like, well

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:18.338
I think investment banking is going
through a

00:45:18.338 --> 00:45:21.362
major transformation, it, it's, it, the
banking industry

00:45:21.362 --> 00:45:23.890
as whole, as a whole is going through it.

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:26.970
One of the things that was always
interesting in, investment

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:30.930
banking, and you could argue that,
compensation has been out-of-whack

00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:33.690
and so forth, that the top person in the
investment

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:37.760
bank very rarely was the most highly paid
person, right.

00:45:37.760 --> 00:45:39.450
I mean you had, you just had wide
variation.

00:45:39.450 --> 00:45:43.520
So I think you'll see a broader view about
that.

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:44.970
My own personal view and this is more a

00:45:44.970 --> 00:45:47.740
political view is I, I actually think
we're gonna

00:45:47.740 --> 00:45:52.940
see I, I think the excess's are gonna have

00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:56.220
to come be, be, be taken back either by
redu.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:59.860
I think it's very difficult to say you
can't have more than 300 times,

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:03.830
I don't believe in forcing it but I, I'm
actually a believer in tax.

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:08.715
I, I actually think we should pay more
tax, may seem like a non, but I,

00:46:08.715 --> 00:46:11.076
I think you, someone has to pay for

00:46:11.076 --> 00:46:14.681
that dislocation of job, government can't
do it.

00:46:14.681 --> 00:46:21.364
And, and so someone's gonna have to pay
for it, and so i think either way that the

00:46:21.364 --> 00:46:24.704
net if you will, is gonna, is gonna come

00:46:24.704 --> 00:46:28.859
closer together for it to be more vibrant
system.

00:46:28.859 --> 00:46:33.720
You know, that's a, that's a personal
view.

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:34.500
Yeah.

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:37.820
&gt;&gt; How do you see the the, kind of the,
overcoming the scarcity

00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:40.100
mentality around the resource issues that
you

00:46:40.100 --> 00:46:42.500
pointed out with water and other things?

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:44.040
How do you see that evolving?

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:47.810
I mean, hopefully it doesn't come to
warfare but, how can we get ahead of that?

00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:52.220
You know as nations or communities to
where, you know, both land, arable

00:46:52.220 --> 00:46:57.190
land being brought up and water resources,
how do you see that playing out,

00:46:57.190 --> 00:46:59.900
is there a good scenario that this could
turn out to be a

00:46:59.900 --> 00:47:03.520
happy ending rather than some of the
negative paths that it could go down?

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:05.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah I'm, I'm hope, I'm hopeful and
hoping that we're

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:10.880
gonna actually add another dimension to
our metrics which is natural capital.

00:47:10.880 --> 00:47:13.710
We, we don't actually measure that if you,
if you will.

00:47:13.710 --> 00:47:17.180
I mean, it's an externality.

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:18.560
So this is a, and, and what I mean by that

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:21.300
is our, you know, the carbon that we're
putting into the

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:24.880
air or how efficiently we're using water,
how much water we

00:47:24.880 --> 00:47:28.410
have, I think there's a notion of natural
capital, that isn't there.

00:47:28.410 --> 00:47:30.320
And what I think is very interesting in
Davos, I

00:47:30.320 --> 00:47:33.700
was in a working session with some people
from Standard

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:35.780
and Poor and the United Nations, a guy
named Hawken

00:47:35.780 --> 00:47:38.700
Steiner, who's doing a lot of work on, on
natural capital.

00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:42.860
And how can we put that into bond ratings,
right?

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:45.480
So we, we look at a country let's say,
like Canada

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:48.780
where I'm from and we would actually look
at the supply

00:47:48.780 --> 00:47:51.980
and demand of water or resources that are,
that are critical

00:47:51.980 --> 00:47:55.400
and that actually gets factored into the
bond rating, if you will.

00:47:55.400 --> 00:47:58.110
I'm, I'm, I'm not giving the details
enough and it's not

00:47:58.110 --> 00:48:02.010
worked out but there's a sense of pricing
that into things.

00:48:02.010 --> 00:48:04.980
Right, on the natural capital side and I
think.

00:48:04.980 --> 00:48:06.960
I think we're gonna have to go that way.

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:08.870
The other one, the other piece of work
that I'm very

00:48:08.870 --> 00:48:12.200
excited about is work done by Ellen
MacArthur, you know the

00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:14.900
woman who sailed around the world in the
Vendee Globe, she's

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:19.010
got this foundation and they're calling it
the circular economy, right.

00:48:19.010 --> 00:48:24.170
Which is basically requiring businesses,
especially manufacturers.

00:48:24.170 --> 00:48:26.630
To think more deliberately about what

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:29.890
proportion of that material will be
recycled.

00:48:29.890 --> 00:48:33.110
So if you, if you're making a cell phone,
you

00:48:33.110 --> 00:48:35.480
actually have a standard that says that
those parts are

00:48:35.480 --> 00:48:37.940
made such that when you finish using the
cell phone,

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:42.160
you return it to the people you bought it
from.

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:44.850
And it's those parts are, 85% of those
parts

00:48:44.850 --> 00:48:47.830
are used again even if it's in some new
technology.

00:48:47.830 --> 00:48:50.140
And today I'm, I'm giving you a very
gutball

00:48:50.140 --> 00:48:53.990
view because it depends by metal and by
natural resource.

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:58.830
We're, we're, we're recycling about 5% of
what we do, so there's a huge upside and

00:48:58.830 --> 00:49:01.330
I think, that's again where some
regulation may

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:04.070
come in, so there's pricing and there's
regulation.

00:49:04.070 --> 00:49:05.240
And I think we'll have to get there,

00:49:05.240 --> 00:49:08.120
because if we don't, the con, consequences
aren't good.

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:10.460
My, again from a very personal point of
view, I think a

00:49:10.460 --> 00:49:14.440
lot World War II, and you look at what was
happening with Japan.

00:49:14.440 --> 00:49:18.560
And they, they were going through their
own version of urbanization

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:21.820
and a growth in the middle class that had
to be fed.

00:49:21.820 --> 00:49:27.110
They had resource problems in Asia, and
there were tensions

00:49:27.110 --> 00:49:30.420
around where to find that rubber and oil
and so forth.

00:49:30.420 --> 00:49:32.200
And I'm not saying that's the reason why
we

00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:35.400
had that, that conflict but it certainly
played the part.

00:49:35.400 --> 00:49:38.240
And I think if, if you are leading a
country and

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:44.850
you've got 500,000 people coming into
Shanghai every year and those people

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:47.600
need to be fed, and you don't have enough
food to

00:49:47.600 --> 00:49:51.100
be able to feed them, you want to find
security of supply.

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:54.240
And so I think it's a, I don't think we'll

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:56.070
get to that outcome but that if we don't
that's the

00:49:56.070 --> 00:49:59.890
unintended consequence, I think we'll get
a lot of tension

00:49:59.890 --> 00:50:03.450
because there just wont be enough
resources to, to do it.

00:50:06.210 --> 00:50:10.185
Oh sorry, why don't we go up there and
then to you, is that, yeah.

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:14.810
&gt;&gt; Name is Colin Supco, thank you very
much for being here.

00:50:14.810 --> 00:50:16.630
So I wanted to talk to you and ask a

00:50:16.630 --> 00:50:20.920
question about the personal attribute side
of your circle here.

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:25.660
As a world class consulting company, what
are you doing to address the

00:50:25.660 --> 00:50:31.790
personal attribute situation other than
talking to executives about, hey,

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:35.210
this is, this is the type of new leaders
you need

00:50:35.210 --> 00:50:39.610
to bringing up in order to survive in this
boot cat environment?

00:50:39.610 --> 00:50:43.660
And have you seen any models that have
been successful in in making that happen?

00:50:44.870 --> 00:50:48.800
&gt;&gt; Great question and I, I think, what
what, what

00:50:48.800 --> 00:50:51.080
we are definitely, we're and I wouldn't
say we're there yet.

00:50:51.080 --> 00:50:54.970
But we've just done a complete review of
our people development side of things.

00:50:54.970 --> 00:50:58.676
I just say one thing we, in McKinsey I
think, McKinsey alum,

00:50:58.676 --> 00:51:03.540
there are 192 CEOs of billion dollar plus
companies that come from Mckinsey.

00:51:03.540 --> 00:51:05.088
And I, and, I, that's a very important

00:51:05.088 --> 00:51:07.090
metric for me, that if we actually
generate CEO's.

00:51:07.090 --> 00:51:09.820
I'd like people to stay in Mckinsey, but
the fact

00:51:09.820 --> 00:51:12.710
of the matter is that 90% of the people
