together from around the world that are
actually helping.

00:53:23.430 --> 00:53:26.170
They're basically running a Telecom
company in Afghanistan.

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:31.150
And they're doing it on four week and
eight week efforts as they go through.

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:32.110
It's their own thing.

00:53:32.110 --> 00:53:33.100
They decided they wanted to do it.

00:53:33.100 --> 00:53:35.880
The only thing I worry about is risk
management that, that we, you know,

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:38.720
they don't get, they don't get kidnapped
or whatever and how we deal with that.

00:53:38.720 --> 00:53:41.300
But they've just decided they wanna do
that and so giving

00:53:41.300 --> 00:53:45.020
people more freedom, if you will, in terms
of what they do.

00:53:45.020 --> 00:53:47.170
Cuz, the thing I worry about, in, in
McKinsey and

00:53:47.170 --> 00:53:49.770
other places, we work hard, I'm not, I
don't wanna,

00:53:49.770 --> 00:53:52.600
people work very hard, and there's a
danger that all

00:53:52.600 --> 00:53:56.500
you do is work, and then you become a
boring idiot.

00:53:56.500 --> 00:53:59.520
If all you do is work, and you don't have
hobbies, or

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:03.610
you don't have, you, you're not gonna be a
very effective leader.

00:54:03.610 --> 00:54:05.337
And so that's another, I'm just saying an

00:54:05.337 --> 00:54:07.820
element that, that, that that we've put
in.

00:54:07.820 --> 00:54:10.740
The other thing that we're doing is we
have an effort going on

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:15.130
with four of our clients, where we're
exploring what we think new leadership is.

00:54:15.130 --> 00:54:18.930
And we're taking, we took a combined group
of people to Normandy.

00:54:18.930 --> 00:54:21.630
This may seem like a strange thing to do
and say.

00:54:21.630 --> 00:54:25.900
What, what would you have done if you were
going onto Normandy Beach.

00:54:25.900 --> 00:54:29.030
And you don't have, there isn't actually a
battle plan for some.

00:54:29.030 --> 00:54:33.670
And how do you, how did those guys think
about what they're actually gonna do.

00:54:33.670 --> 00:54:36.380
We took the group to the Fukushima, you
know

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:39.252
if you think about the earthquake that's
gone on there.

00:54:39.252 --> 00:54:41.127
Or to Ochaya and said you guys are

00:54:41.127 --> 00:54:44.470
now leading the response to actually
what's happened.

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:46.050
What would you actually do?

00:54:46.050 --> 00:54:49.523
And then we've created scenarios where
there's disasters that

00:54:49.523 --> 00:54:51.740
we, you know, that we, we, we made up.

00:54:51.740 --> 00:54:55.810
What if someone, what if there's a
terrorist attack on the Visa data center?

00:54:55.810 --> 00:55:00.930
And if you think about what the credit
card information, and what

00:55:00.930 --> 00:55:04.560
that, that would create a disaster from an
economic point of view.

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:08.030
Well, so, what is it, you guys have two
days to be able to figure it out.

00:55:08.030 --> 00:55:11.510
And then we have them actually make a

00:55:11.510 --> 00:55:15.480
presentation, to, you know, the head of
that organization,

00:55:15.480 --> 00:55:17.970
to the National Security Council, just to
some different

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:20.740
people, so we're trying, we're learning if
you will.

00:55:20.740 --> 00:55:22.270
And what are some of the skills that would
make

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:25.334
you better at that and, and how you do it?

00:55:25.334 --> 00:55:28.410
And so this is, I'd say that's the
exploration side, and then

00:55:28.410 --> 00:55:31.550
the commitment is we got to then rewire
what it is we do.

00:55:31.550 --> 00:55:33.660
What are we training people to do?

00:55:33.660 --> 00:55:36.820
How many different experiences do we give
people?

00:55:36.820 --> 00:55:39.670
I'm in the mode now with, we have to, I,
I'm in the mode of we

00:55:39.670 --> 00:55:42.130
should be dropping people in more pools,
even

00:55:42.130 --> 00:55:44.800
if we don't think they can swim, right.

00:55:44.800 --> 00:55:47.210
So I'll give one, one specific example.

00:55:47.210 --> 00:55:49.175
I met with president of Azure, Bajan.

00:55:49.175 --> 00:55:51.300
Who's a very interesting guy, wants to

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:53.610
turn this country into all sorts of
things.

00:55:53.610 --> 00:55:55.369
Our normal process of opening up an office

00:55:55.369 --> 00:55:58.230
there, we'd take a senior person from
Mckinsey.

00:55:58.230 --> 00:56:00.780
We'd have them be flying in and talking to
people and so forth.

00:56:00.780 --> 00:56:03.980
What we're gonna do is take a three year

00:56:03.980 --> 00:56:06.570
Mackenzie person who's there and said, you
know what?

00:56:06.570 --> 00:56:08.280
You're moving to Baku.

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:11.460
Sorry, that's, you're, gonna be your home
for the next couple of years.

00:56:11.460 --> 00:56:14.460
And your job is to build a practice, build
a network.

00:56:14.460 --> 00:56:16.680
You can bring the people in you wanna do.

00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:17.130
You do it.

00:56:17.130 --> 00:56:18.680
You're going for two years.

00:56:18.680 --> 00:56:19.860
Go for it.

00:56:19.860 --> 00:56:22.110
Right and get, we're, we're doing this
actually in, in Ghana.

00:56:22.110 --> 00:56:24.730
I mean there's, and, again, I'm not trying
to show,

00:56:24.730 --> 00:56:27.620
well, for the emerging countries we don't
care so we'll.

00:56:27.620 --> 00:56:30.980
It's more about, these are the greatest
growth opportunities to be able to do it.

00:56:30.980 --> 00:56:33.620
And so I think we gotta, if you will, if I
could call it,

00:56:33.620 --> 00:56:38.440
fling people into the pool more with, and
then discover if they can swim.

00:56:38.440 --> 00:56:41.398
We, we'll obviously try to help them if
they can't, but I, that's the.

00:56:41.398 --> 00:56:43.518
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:56:43.518 --> 00:56:45.270
Probably, one more question.

00:56:47.250 --> 00:56:49.550
I think it was, yeah, here.

00:56:49.550 --> 00:56:51.150
&gt;&gt; So we talked about China quite a

00:56:51.150 --> 00:56:52.600
bit and, and, you know, also resource
conflict.

00:56:52.600 --> 00:56:54.880
You know, when you look at the current
conflict with, with Japan in

00:56:54.880 --> 00:56:56.590
the East China Sea, you know, maybe

00:56:56.590 --> 00:56:59.250
not purely rationally explained by
resource conflict.

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:00.420
How do you, like, how do you see that
through

00:57:00.420 --> 00:57:01.810
the lens of all the time that you've spent
there?

00:57:03.180 --> 00:57:06.410
Why should I look here and I should defer
to my colleagues but I'll tell,

00:57:06.410 --> 00:57:08.560
I, I am, that's a conflict that I

00:57:08.560 --> 00:57:11.780
actually really worry about on the
geopolitical side.

00:57:11.780 --> 00:57:15.030
I mean there's lots of thing, you could
worry about Iran, we

00:57:15.030 --> 00:57:20.630
could, we could worry about you know,
North Africa and what's going on.

00:57:20.630 --> 00:57:24.550
I actually do worry about the, that, that
conflict, and

00:57:24.550 --> 00:57:26.570
I think it is, to your point, it's more
than just

00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:32.440
the natural resources that are around
those islands and I,

00:57:32.440 --> 00:57:35.320
I think what we can't forget is some of
the history.

00:57:35.320 --> 00:57:36.950
And this is probably going to be very

00:57:36.950 --> 00:57:38.800
politically correct, and I've got my
colleagues here,

00:57:38.800 --> 00:57:42.420
can throw something at me, or say it's
wrong, and I'll just give you one story.

00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:47.270
I've, I've had a, which is, I think the
emotional side that's there.

00:57:47.270 --> 00:57:52.330
And I think that the, the need to be able
to kinda build more

00:57:52.330 --> 00:57:54.490
bridges at all sorts of different levels

00:57:54.490 --> 00:57:57.090
especially between China and Japan is very
important.

00:57:57.090 --> 00:57:58.420
Because of the history that's going on.

00:57:58.420 --> 00:58:00.030
And the story, I'll just tell you this
quickly

00:58:00.030 --> 00:58:01.647
and then I, it's probably not a very good
story.

00:58:01.647 --> 00:58:03.780
We had a summer associate conference in

00:58:05.900 --> 00:58:07.220
Singapore.

00:58:07.220 --> 00:58:09.350
And we had, there were two Chinese

00:58:09.350 --> 00:58:11.830
summer students, students from there, from
Harvard, actually.

00:58:11.830 --> 00:58:13.800
There were two from Taiwan and two from
Japan.

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:19.130
We were having dinner together, and I
looked at the Chinese friends and I said,

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:23.890
what would you do if Taiwan seceded, said
we're not gonna be part of China?

00:58:23.890 --> 00:58:26.260
What was probably politically incorrect,
but what the hell, well

00:58:26.260 --> 00:58:30.760
so, what, what if, what would you do if
Taiwan seceded?

00:58:30.760 --> 00:58:34.278
And there's one, Chinese woman who was
working with us, said, we go to war.

00:58:34.278 --> 00:58:36.230
I mean they're part of the motherland, I
said

00:58:36.230 --> 00:58:38.550
come on you, you would go to, she goes,
absolutely,

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:40.370
that's, they're part of the, they are part
of

00:58:40.370 --> 00:58:43.170
China and, that, she goes, I like these
two guys.

00:58:43.170 --> 00:58:44.100
They're my friends.

00:58:44.100 --> 00:58:44.900
They're from Taiwan.

00:58:44.900 --> 00:58:46.840
I like them, but that's the situation.

00:58:46.840 --> 00:58:49.120
I went oh okay, that, that's interesting.

00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:50.370
And I said what about Japan?

00:58:51.450 --> 00:58:56.690
And this one particular woman said I, I
hate the Japanese.

00:58:56.690 --> 00:58:59.030
And I went holy god, I said now hate

00:58:59.030 --> 00:59:01.930
is probably too harsh of a, that's an
English word.

00:59:01.930 --> 00:59:03.700
You don't, that's not really a word.

00:59:03.700 --> 00:59:05.520
She goes, now I know exactly what that
word means.

00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:08.580
Now, I like those two guys, they're great.

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:12.130
But I don't like what's being done before
and where it is.

00:59:12.130 --> 00:59:14.430
And that, that to me, this is not a very
good example.

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:17.560
It's too anecdotal, but I'm just saying,
there, I think there's

00:59:17.560 --> 00:59:21.030
a lot of emotion and, and I think it's
going to be

00:59:21.030 --> 00:59:25.880
very important that there be multiple
connection points between, you know

00:59:25.880 --> 00:59:29.670
its high, the high school level, the
business level, the social level.

00:59:29.670 --> 00:59:31.510
So people can really get to know each
other.

00:59:31.510 --> 00:59:34.800
And we don't get the nationalism on all
sides.

00:59:34.800 --> 00:59:38.568
And again ch, maybe you guys could give me
a thumbs down or whether.

00:59:38.568 --> 00:59:40.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You think I'm right on how you feel.

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:42.000
But I think there's, I don't think

00:59:42.000 --> 00:59:45.900
we should underestimate the emotional
issues that are

00:59:45.900 --> 00:59:50.160
there and that they have to be dealt with
as we, as we go through it.

00:59:50.160 --> 00:59:53.280
And I, it's interesting because I lived in
Korea for six years.

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:56.950
I would say that there's less tensions
between Korea and Japan, I

00:59:56.950 --> 00:59:58.660
think there's actually quite a good

00:59:58.660 --> 01:00:00.890
relationship over time and how that's
worked.

01:00:00.890 --> 01:00:05.030
I don't think it's as developed between
China and Japan.

01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:08.260
So I think we have to be careful about
these conflicts.

01:00:08.260 --> 01:00:10.740
And the, what I would, what's interesting,
right.

01:00:10.740 --> 01:00:13.050
And I don't mean to sound, this sounds
like, I don't want to end on

01:00:13.050 --> 01:00:16.810
a down note but if you, we're coming up to
the anniversary of world war one.

01:00:16.810 --> 01:00:20.080
And I don't know how many historians are
here.

01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:23.150
But if you think about how that war
started, the, there

01:00:23.150 --> 01:00:27.160
were accidents, a series of accidents in
some ways that occurred.

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:30.083
And what I worry about in geopolitical
side is

01:00:30.083 --> 01:00:35.170
what if a US naval boat runs over, runs,

01:00:35.170 --> 01:00:39.040
you know, runs over six North Korean
fishing boats

01:00:39.040 --> 01:00:40.960
somewhere near those islands when, you
know what I mean?

01:00:40.960 --> 01:00:44.070
You just can, and that's why I think
communications is gonna be key.

01:00:45.090 --> 01:00:48.220
And that's why I think it's wonderful when
we have

01:00:48.220 --> 01:00:51.210
at the university level and high school
level, much more interaction.

01:00:51.210 --> 01:00:53.070
So there's a deep social linkage.

01:00:53.070 --> 01:00:55.158
So people can say, I understand that isn't
how those people are.

01:00:55.158 --> 01:00:58.860
And you know, probably, I didn't wanna end

01:00:58.860 --> 01:01:01.860
on a emotional thing like that, on the
conflict.

01:01:01.860 --> 01:01:04.130
But I do think we have to realize that
with a

01:01:04.130 --> 01:01:08.476
world that's more volatile, and there will
be scarcities and so forth.

01:01:08.476 --> 01:01:11.620
And also in equality, there are very very

01:01:11.620 --> 01:01:13.920
rich people and there are very very poor
people.

01:01:13.920 --> 01:01:15.760
And the very, very poor people with

01:01:15.760 --> 01:01:18.120
technology now know how the very, very
rich

01:01:18.120 --> 01:01:22.620
people live, and some of them aren't
exactly happy to see how that, that works.

01:01:22.620 --> 01:01:26.290
And so I think as business leaders we need

01:01:26.290 --> 01:01:29.870
to play a broader role in working with
that.

01:01:29.870 --> 01:01:32.190
That's not up to the government to fix.

01:01:32.190 --> 01:01:36.575
I think we should, we should play a role
in it.

01:01:36.575 --> 01:01:38.567
Anyhow, I'll stop at that point.

01:01:38.567 --> 01:01:38.617
&gt;&gt; [SOUND].

01:01:38.617 --> 01:01:41.534
&gt;&gt; [NOISE] Thanks, thank you.

